 A visit's scientific fields include observation date, observer list, species cover estimates, and notes. Some edits are unrelated, such as correcting an observer name and adding a missing species. Other edits are related, such as revising a species identification and its cover estimate. The server cannot infer all such relationships from field names.
 
-The selected concurrency policy will define the remaining submission metadata and review operations. They are deliberately not specified yet.
+### Concurrency Policy: Whole-Visit Revisions
+
+SELECTED: each edit names its starting revision. Concurrent edits produce competing complete revisions for a coordinator to resolve. This preserves the worker's submitted visit as a unit and needs relatively little merge logic, but even edits to unrelated fields create review work. Tablet-sync will retain base revision IDs and display pending competing revisions. Review-export will need a revision comparison workflow and exclude unresolved visits from its export set.
+
+The alternative (field-aware three-way merge) was considered and not selected. It reduces review volume and gets more visits ready for export, but requires maintained rules for scalar fields, observer lists, and species entries. Automatically combined fields can also cross scientific relationships the rules do not understand. The team prefers a bigger review queue over maintaining merge rules that can combine scientifically related edits without noticing.
 
 ## Submission and Retrieval
 
 Authenticate the submitting worker and validate field types and plot references before accepting a submission. Preserve the original payload separately from any later resolved or combined representation. A retry with the same key and identical payload returns the original acceptance result; reuse with a different payload is rejected.
 
-Retrieval must distinguish a usable current state from unresolved review work. Exactly how those states are represented remains part of the open choice.
+Each submission includes the base revision ID it was derived from. The server assigns a monotonically increasing revision number per visit on each accepted submission.
 
-## Open Design Choice: Concurrent Visit Edits
+Retrieval distinguishes three states: clean (single current revision with no competing submissions), competing (multiple submissions at the same revision level awaiting resolution), and resolved (a coordinator has chosen a winning revision or produced a combined revision, and that revision is now current). Unresolved competing revisions are excluded from the clean current-state response used by exports.
 
-OPEN: select the concurrency policy before completing this node's public contract.
+## Review Operations
 
-Two approaches remain credible:
+A coordinator resolves competing revisions by selecting one submitted revision as the winner, or by creating a new combined revision from multiple submissions. The resolution records the coordinator's identity, the contributing submission IDs, and the chosen or combined revision content. The resolved revision becomes the new current revision for that visit.
 
-- Whole-visit revisions: each edit names its starting revision. Concurrent edits produce competing complete revisions for a coordinator to resolve. This preserves the worker's submitted visit as a unit and needs relatively little merge logic, but even edits to unrelated fields create review work. Tablet-sync would retain base revision IDs and display pending competing revisions. Review-export would need a revision comparison workflow and exclude unresolved visits from its export set.
-- Field-aware three-way merge: each edit supplies its base state and changes. Disjoint field changes combine automatically; overlapping changes require review. This reduces review volume and gets more visits ready for export, but requires maintained rules for scalar fields, observer lists, and species entries. Automatically combined fields can also cross scientific relationships the rules do not understand. Tablet-sync would retain base snapshots and send structured changes. Review-export would need field-level provenance and a review view for overlapping changes.
+## Decisions & Alternatives
 
-The choice trades review workload and submission size against implementation burden and preservation of whole-visit intent. Neither approach may discard original submissions. Field-aware merging is not ruled out by a requirement that every combined record receive manual approval.
+| Decision | Rationale | Alternatives Considered |
+|---|---|---|
+| Whole-visit revisions over field-aware three-way merge | Preserves submitted visit as a unit; simpler merge logic (no field-level rules to maintain); team prefers a bigger review queue over maintaining merge rules that can combine scientifically related edits without noticing; original submissions are never discarded; coordinator sees exactly what each worker submitted | Field-aware three-way merge: combines disjoint field changes automatically (e.g., observer name fix + species addition), reducing review volume and getting more visits export-ready; but requires maintained rules for scalar fields, observer lists (append vs replace), species entries (merge by species code), and cover estimates; automatically combined fields can cross scientific relationships the rules do not understand (e.g., changing species ID and its cover estimate separately gets merged into an incoherent record); every combined record would still need manual approval per requirements, so the review reduction is partial |
 
 ## Downstream Contract Work
 
-After selection, define the submission envelope, current-state response, unresolved-work representation, and resolution operation here. Tablet-sync will size its local storage and retry state machine around that envelope. Review-export will size its coordinator interface and export eligibility rules around the unresolved-work representation. Both must link back to this node rather than invent their own concurrency semantics.
+### Tablet-sync must accommodate:
+- Local storage of base revision ID per drafted visit (to include on submission)
+- Retry state machine that reuses the same idempotency key on connection failure
+- Display of pending competing revisions when the server returns competing state
+- Conflict UI: worker sees their submitted revision alongside other competing revisions for the same visit, can choose to re-edit from the current revision or wait for coordinator resolution
+- Offline draft retention across tablet restarts and multi-day disconnection (HLD Goal 1)
+
+### Review-export must accommodate:
+- Revision comparison screen: side-by-side view of all competing revisions for a visit, highlighting field differences
+- Coordinator actions: select one revision as winner, or create a combined revision by picking field values from multiple submissions
+- Resolution recording: coordinator ID, contributing submission IDs, and the chosen/combined content
+- Export eligibility: only visits in clean or resolved state enter the Friday CSV; competing visits are held out
+- CSV output matches partner's existing columns exactly (HLD Goal 6)
+- Provenance trail: exported values trace back through resolution to original submissions (HLD Tenet 3)
+
+### Both must link back to this node rather than invent their own concurrency semantics.
